Setting, continued from the previous page. A^•_* is a cochain complex of (co)simplicial k-modules: for each n there is a simplicial k-module m ↦ A^n_m, and the differential d runs in the cohomological index. The model case is polynomial (here, divided-power) differential n-forms on the algebraic simplices Δ^m. The target is the complex of simplicial modules C^n_{*M}: m ↦ C^n(Δ^m, M), the M-valued n-cochains on the standard m-simplex, with the simplicial-cochain differential.

Step 1 (previous page). C^n(Δ^•, M) is the coinduced (right Kan extended) object Map(Δ([n], •), M). So a map of simplicial modules u_n : A^n_* → C^n_{*M} is the same as a single linear map k_n : A^n_n → M, namely evaluation on the fundamental n-simplex. Under this dictionary:
- d_C ∘ u_n corresponds to k_n ∘ ∂_{n+1}, where ∂_{n+1} : A^n_{n+1} → A^n_n is the alternating sum of the face operators (restriction to the boundary of Δ^{n+1}).
- u_{n+1} ∘ d_A corresponds to k_{n+1} ∘ d^n on A^n_{n+1}.

Step 2 (this page). The u_n form a map of complexes if and only if the 'Stokes condition' holds: k_n ∂_{n+1} = k_{n+1} d, as maps A^n_{n+1} → M. If k is thought of as ∫ over the standard simplex, this is literally ∫_{∂Δ} ω = ∫_Δ dω.

Step 3. Given a Stokes system, for any simplicial set X_* ∈ ob Δ̂ one gets a cochain map Γ(X_*, A^•_{X_*}) = Hom(X_*, A^•) → C^•(X_*, M) = Γ(X_*, C^•_M|X_*). Here A_{X_*} is A viewed as a presheaf on the category of simplices of X. This chain-level map represents a derived-category map Γ(X, A_X) → RΓ_X(M), and there is a commutative square:
- top: Γ(X, A_X) → C^•(X, M);
- right: C^•(X, M) ≅ RΓ(X, M);
- left: Γ → RΓ(X, A_X);
- bottom: RΓ(X, A_X) → RΓ(X, M).

Step 4. If A^• is a resolution of the constant M_* (a Poincaré lemma on each simplex) and k_0 restricts to the identity on M ⊂ A^0_0, then the bottom arrow is an isomorphism. The next page adds that when the A^n are Γ-acyclic, the left arrow is an isomorphism too. Integration is then a chain-level realization of the de Rham isomorphism.

Step 5 (next page). Existence criterion for a Stokes system, built degree by degree, under three conditions:
- A^i_n = 0 for i > n;
- H^n(A^•_n) = 0 for n ≥ 1;
- H^{n-1}(A^•_n) = 0 for n ≥ 2.

Purpose: an axiomatic, coefficient-free de Rham theorem for simplicial sets. It is aimed at the divided-power polynomial de Rham complex over an arbitrary base ring (e.g. Z), which is the subject of the 1976 IHÉS talk.
